skillDailyBody:
When the user asks to organize daily notes/jots:
1. Read relevant content with search_notes or read_note first;
2. Categorize into "Today's highlights / Todos / Ideas & inspiration / Excerpts";
3. Create or update today's daily note (a path like /Daily/2026/Jan 2), preserving existing content;
4. Briefly report the changes when done.

skillMeetingBody:
When the user asks to organize meeting notes:
1. Read the raw notes provided by the user;
2. Distill into three sections: topics & discussion points, conclusions reached, action items (with owner and deadline; leave blank if unknown);
3. Write into a new document or append to the document specified by the user;
4. Use the SiYuan todo list syntax * [ ] for action items.

skillSqlBody:
When keyword search (search_notes) is not enough and you need precise statistics, conditional filtering or batch location of notes, query SiYuan's SQLite index directly with query_sql. SELECT only.

Schema essentials:
- blocks: one row per block. Useful columns: id (block id), root_id (owning document id), box (notebook id), hpath (human-readable path), content (plain text), markdown (raw markup), type (block type), subtype, created/updated (14-digit timestamps yyyyMMddHHmmss, comparable as strings), sort (order within doc).
- Common type values: d=document, h=heading, p=paragraph, l=list, i=list item, b=blockquote, s=super block, t=table, c=code block, m=math, av=database, html, embed, audio/video, widget.
- attributes: block attributes with block_id, name, value, etc.; custom attribute names start with custom-. If unsure about columns, probe first: SELECT * FROM attributes LIMIT 3.
- refs: block reference relations; def_block_id = referenced block, root_id/block_id = referencing source.

Common templates:
- Recently updated docs: SELECT id, hpath, updated FROM blocks WHERE type='d' ORDER BY updated DESC LIMIT 20;
- Paragraphs containing a word: SELECT id, root_id, hpath, content FROM blocks WHERE type='p' AND content LIKE '%keyword%' LIMIT 50;
- All blocks of a doc in order: SELECT id, type, content FROM blocks WHERE root_id='<doc id>' ORDER BY sort;
- Blocks with a custom attribute: SELECT block_id, name, value FROM attributes WHERE name='custom-xxx' LIMIT 50;
- Sources referencing a block: SELECT root_id, content FROM refs WHERE def_block_id='<block id>' LIMIT 50;
- Block count per doc: SELECT root_id, COUNT(*) AS n FROM blocks GROUP BY root_id ORDER BY n DESC LIMIT 20;

Notes:
- Statements must start with SELECT; strings use single quotes, escape inner quotes by doubling ('').
- Always add LIMIT and SELECT only needed columns to avoid huge results.
- The SQL index lags writes by seconds; just-written content may not appear yet.

skillBackupBody:
When a task involves batch or destructive operations (bulk deleting docs/blocks/database rows, moving whole folders, mass renaming, large-scale rewriting):
1. Call create_snapshot first, with a memo describing the operation (e.g. "backup before merging tags");
2. Tell the user a snapshot was created and it can be found via list_snapshots and rolled back in Settings → Snapshot;
3. Then perform the batch operations;
4. Small single changes (editing one block, adding one row) do not need a snapshot — do not over-backup.

skillTagsBody:
When the user asks to organize, merge or clean up tags:
1. Use search_tags (empty keyword) to list all tags with usage counts;
2. Identify semantically duplicated or inconsistently written tags (e.g. #AI/#ai/#artificial-intelligence, singular/plural, mixed languages), propose a merge plan (old tag → new tag mapping) and get user confirmation before acting;
3. Merge one by one with rename_tag (renaming updates all references);
4. Remove tags confirmed worthless with remove_tag;
5. Report the merges/removals and suggest a consistent naming style (e.g. all lowercase, single language).

skillOrganizeBody:
When the user asks to organize a folder or archive scattered documents:
1. List documents in the target scope with list_docs (or find scattered docs with search_docs);
2. Sample-read contents with read_note, then propose an archiving plan: target path per document and whether to rename; present the plan as a checklist and wait for user confirmation;
3. After confirmation, move with move_doc and rename with rename_doc;
4. Moving documents does not break existing block references (they resolve by block id) — mention this when reporting;
5. Summarize the changes (old name → new location).
